{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"e60b96bfb4968c23","type":"text","text":"\n<span style=\"font-size: 200px;\">Network Architecture</span>\n#netdesign","x":-480,"y":-595,"width":1200,"height":675,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"a38c6b52f5cefa42","type":"text","text":"Encrypted original IP packet","x":5438,"y":1612,"width":207,"height":68,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"6c6e4ca8f47edc16","type":"text","text":"VPN header","x":5645,"y":1612,"width":128,"height":68,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"633db9962ef709dc","type":"text","text":"IP Header","x":5773,"y":1612,"width":128,"height":68,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"3fd0c18a59878bc4","type":"text","text":"Internet","x":5591,"y":1728,"width":128,"height":50},
		{"id":"43dbf44ef1818d52","type":"text","text":"Office A","x":5820,"y":1728,"width":128,"height":50},
		{"id":"9a82e1d4cae3bd9f","type":"text","text":"IPsec VPN tunnel","x":5556,"y":1868,"width":202,"height":50},
		{"id":"ed06f831a2f0b28a","type":"text","text":"P Router","x":5607,"y":273,"width":117,"height":50},
		{"id":"f100d22dbc3f38db","type":"text","text":"PE Router","x":5724,"y":182,"width":140,"height":50},
		{"id":"afdc808a52ddbb8f","type":"text","text":"OSPF","x":5890,"y":132,"width":97,"height":50},
		{"id":"827739e3372d245b","type":"text","text":"Office B\nCE router","x":5939,"y":273,"width":117,"height":50},
		{"id":"e0eeb66128a6b83d","type":"text","text":"Service Provider","x":5461,"y":587,"width":409,"height":143,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"c2138dd2044faf05","type":"text","text":"Office A \nCE router","x":5313,"y":633,"width":117,"height":50},
		{"id":"5302b0d66f08d60f","type":"text","text":"Office B\nCE router","x":5935,"y":633,"width":117,"height":50},
		{"id":"4167079be109252c","type":"text","text":"OSPF","x":5628,"y":788,"width":97,"height":50},
		{"id":"5628165a0db4d458","type":"text","text":"- CE and PE routers do not form peerings\n- Allows CE routers to directly form OSPF peerings with each other\n- The service provider network is entirely transparent to the CE routers\n- In effect, it is like the two CE routers are directly connected.\n\t- Their WAN interfaces will be in the same subnet\n- If a routing protocol is used, the two CE routers will peer directly with each other\n- As if service provider network is a switch connecting two CE routers together","x":4729,"y":567,"width":461,"height":373},
		{"id":"0380675d0b148306","type":"text","text":"Service Provider","x":5455,"y":18,"width":409,"height":50,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"bf62294279b558d1","type":"text","text":"P Router","x":5595,"y":93,"width":117,"height":50},
		{"id":"325770c49749aac9","type":"text","text":"OSPF","x":5328,"y":132,"width":97,"height":50},
		{"id":"163c07c2f1038ae7","type":"text","text":"PE Router","x":5455,"y":182,"width":140,"height":50},
		{"id":"776924e7c3617b14","type":"text","text":"Office A \nCE router","x":5248,"y":273,"width":117,"height":50},
		{"id":"546f2a64d745bc5a","type":"text","text":"Service Provider","x":5461,"y":359,"width":409,"height":50,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"457d7d63e93cc951","type":"text","text":"MPLS Layer 3 VPN","x":4729,"y":59,"width":461,"height":59,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"41ce2a288670df55","type":"text","text":"- Only PE/P routers use MPLS (CE does not use it)\n- When using a layer 3 MPLS VPN, the CE and PE routers peer using OSPF, for example, to share routing information\n\t- Office A's CE will peer with one PE, and office B's CE will peer with the other PE\n\t- Office A's CE will learn about office B's routes via this OSPF peering, and office B's CE will learn about office A's routes too","x":4728,"y":118,"width":462,"height":271},
		{"id":"5728cba6d1e8c81e","type":"text","text":"MPLS Layer 2 VPN","x":4730,"y":508,"width":460,"height":59,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"cb7594558b7c9b75","type":"text","text":"- A VPN between two devices and is used to connect two sites together over the internet\n- A VPN 'tunnel' is created between the two devices by encapsulating the original IP packet with a VPN header and a new IP header.\n\t- When using IPsec, the original packet is encrypted before being encapsulated with the new header\n- Unencrypted data is sent to a router which encrypts the data, adds a VPN header, and a new IP header. Encrypted data is sent over the tunnel. The receiving router decrypts the and sends it to the endhost","x":3918,"y":1587,"width":587,"height":388},
		{"id":"00d6fc5597bb0f76","type":"text","text":"Packet Sending Process","x":4622,"y":1562,"width":599,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"fb3b401f16f3addd","type":"text","text":"1. The sending device combines the original packet and session key (encryption key) and runs them through and encryption formula\n2. The sending device encapsulates the encrypted packet with a VPN header and a new IP header\n3. The sending device sends the new packet to the device on the other side of the tunnel\n4. The receiving device decrypts the data to get the original packet, and then forwards the original packet to its destination\n- In a site to site VPN a tunnel is formed only between two tunnel end points (ex: the two routers connected to the internet)\n- All other devices in each site don't need to create a VPN for themselves. They can send unencrypted data to their site's router, which will encrypt it and forward it in the tunnel ass described above","x":4622,"y":1612,"width":599,"height":388},
		{"id":"d07356935a0d47bb","type":"text","text":"Office A","x":5374,"y":1728,"width":128,"height":50},
		{"id":"81510837b7b5173c","type":"text","text":"Summary","x":3393,"y":2035,"width":407,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"97cd1f109143d7ff","type":"text","text":"TLS VPN TUNNEL","x":4800,"y":1042,"width":122,"height":84},
		{"id":"e82293a5be9f4347","type":"text","text":"TLS VPN TUNNEL","x":4800,"y":1146,"width":122,"height":84},
		{"id":"a950ce5804db8f82","type":"text","text":"Firewall/Router","x":4982,"y":1163,"width":164,"height":50},
		{"id":"a52d947f7ac48940","type":"text","text":"Server1","x":5201,"y":1042,"width":129,"height":50},
		{"id":"a5798fcd5115a2c2","type":"text","text":"Server2","x":5206,"y":1163,"width":124,"height":50},
		{"id":"7a5027dbce570922","type":"text","text":"Server3","x":5206,"y":1293,"width":124,"height":50},
		{"id":"8379cfee1fe106e9","type":"text","text":"TLS VPN TUNNEL","x":4800,"y":1259,"width":122,"height":84},
		{"id":"1757ddc0450bf06b","type":"text","text":"Internet VPNs","x":3391,"y":1252,"width":407,"height":50,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"3daf311788e66970","type":"text","text":"- Whereas site-to-site VPNs are used to make a point-to-point connection between two sites over the Internet, remote-access VPNs are used to allow end devices (PCs, mobile phones) to access the company's intenral resources securely over the internet\n- Remote-access VPNs typically use TLS (Transport Layer Security)\n\t- TLS is also what provides security for HTTPS (HTTP Secure)\n\t- TLS was formerly known as SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) and developed by Netscape, but it was renamed to TLS when it was standardized by IETF\n- VPN client software (ex: Cisco AnyConnect) is installed on end devices ( for example company-provided laptops that employees use to work from home)\n- These end devices form secure tunnels to one of the company's routers/firewalls acting as a TLS Server\n- This allows the end users to securely access resources on the company's internal network without being directly connected to the company network","x":3918,"y":960,"width":587,"height":470},
		{"id":"31daf90e7ea621ab","type":"text","text":"PC1","x":4660,"y":1059,"width":102,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"6de2910e0de87deb","type":"text","text":"PC2","x":4660,"y":1163,"width":102,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2915200fa0294aa5","type":"text","text":"PC3","x":4660,"y":1276,"width":102,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8080a8613f651e71","type":"text","text":"- Private WAN services such as leased lines and MPLS provide security because each customer's traffic is separated by using dedicated physical connections (leased lines) or by MPLS tags.\n- When using the internet as a WAN to connect sites together, there is no built-in security by default.\n- To provide secure communications over the internet, VPNs (virtual Private Networks) are used.\n- Two kinds of Internet VPNs are covered","x":3391,"y":1302,"width":407,"height":388},
		{"id":"26fc2c17804bfcfb","type":"text","text":"Site-to-Site VPNs using IPsec","x":3918,"y":1537,"width":587,"height":50,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"dc010813e655f9c1","type":"text","text":"Remote-access VPNs using TLS","x":3918,"y":910,"width":587,"height":50,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"51f749ccf758dd51","type":"text","text":"MPLS - Multi Protocol Label Switching","x":4097,"y":132,"width":460,"height":74,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"067b004fd3113381","type":"text","text":"- Similar to the Internet, service providers' MPLS networks are shared infrastructure to make WAN connections.\n- However, the label switching in the name of MPLS allows VPNs to be created over the MPLS infrastructure through the use of labels.\n- Important terms:\n\t- CE router = Customer edge router\n\t\t- connected to PE router\n\t- PE router = Provider Edge router\n\t\t- connects to P routers\n\t- P router = Provider core router\n- When the PE routers receives frames from the CE routers, they add a label to the frame\n- These labels are used to make forwarding decisions within the service provider network, not the destination\n- Many different technologies can be used to conect a service provider's MPLS network for WAN service\n\t- one can be using ethernet (fiber), 4G/5G, CATV (cable TV), or Serial and the other can be using another one.\n","x":4097,"y":206,"width":460,"height":607},
		{"id":"96ff91ab618fd693","type":"text","text":"WAN over shared infrastructure","x":3395,"y":295,"width":407,"height":58,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"9a4d54d9daf4782d","type":"text","text":"- To send traffic between sites the internet can be used for the Enterprises WAN's connections between sites\n- The internet is public so sending data over the internet is unsafe\n- To send traffic between sites, the company will set up VPNs (IPsec VPN tunnel) - packets will be encrypted so only the intended recipients can read it.\n- The encrypted packet is encapsulated within a new packet and sent over the internet","x":3395,"y":353,"width":407,"height":310},
		{"id":"769bebb94a884a16","type":"text","text":"1. GRE over IPsec","x":4639,"y":2083,"width":599,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"94ccd197138977b3","type":"text","text":"- GRE (generic routing encapsulation) creates tunnels like IPsec, however it does not encrypt the original packet, so it is not secure.\n- It has the advantage of being able to encapsulate a wide variety of Layer 3 protocols as well as broadcast and multicast messages\n- To get the flexibility of GRE with the security of IPsec = 'GRE over IPsec' can be used\n- The original packet will be encapsulated by a GRE header and a new IP header, and then the GRE packet will be encrypted and encapsulated within an IPsec VPN header and a new ip header","x":4639,"y":2133,"width":599,"height":288},
		{"id":"020facecaf9acd57","type":"text","text":"2. DMVPN - Dynamic Multipoint VPN","x":4639,"y":2497,"width":599,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"4e29fd511078ed31","type":"text","text":"Step 1: Configure IPsec tunnels to a hub site ","x":5318,"y":2497,"width":479,"height":63,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2f21a9b096471c6b","type":"text","text":"Original Packet + GRE header + New IP header","x":5322,"y":2083,"width":180,"height":101,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3a3e73847794c585","type":"text","text":"Encrypted GRE Packet encapsulated by IPsec VPN header + New IP header","x":5322,"y":2243,"width":180,"height":138,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"422e82adbe559ebe","type":"text","text":"- Cisco developed solution that allows routers to dynamically create a full mesh of IPsec tunnels without having to manually configure every single tunnel\n- Provides the configuration simplicity of hub-and-spoke ( each spoke router only needs one tunnel configured) and the efficiency of direct spoke-to-spoke communication (spoke routers can communicate directly without traffic passing through the hub)","x":4639,"y":2547,"width":599,"height":264},
		{"id":"782719de87a1a841","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5325,"y":2842,"width":130,"height":63},
		{"id":"0a1ce47c35d0da14","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5650,"y":2842,"width":130,"height":63},
		{"id":"c03ce605fd4a6239","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5913,"y":2842,"width":130,"height":63,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"00489df1ded8d640","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":6317,"y":2842,"width":126,"height":63,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"2ab087db7dd7ccff","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5493,"y":2953,"width":130,"height":63},
		{"id":"704dfb850c6f4ede","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":6115,"y":2953,"width":130,"height":63,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"87c6ba8199a3db16","type":"text","text":"IPsec VPN Header","x":5922,"y":2243,"width":181,"height":138,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8a81505125f98256","type":"text","text":"Step 2: The Hub Router gives each router information about how to form an IPsec tunnel with the other routers","x":5941,"y":2497,"width":479,"height":63,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"55ea3f5709156745","type":"text","text":"IP Header","x":6103,"y":2243,"width":181,"height":138,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"d5345ebf27b79cba","type":"text","text":"R (hub)","x":6115,"y":2602,"width":132,"height":63,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"9e77cc0893df0566","type":"text","text":"IP packet","x":5542,"y":2083,"width":120,"height":101,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"bf06712a7a8db58a","type":"text","text":"Encrypted original IP packet","x":5542,"y":2243,"width":380,"height":138,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"4470c471d594b7de","type":"text","text":"GRE Header","x":5662,"y":2083,"width":140,"height":101,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"31c84f2f9f8d987b","type":"text","text":"IP Header","x":5802,"y":2083,"width":120,"height":101,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"65e37760b8512f3a","type":"text","text":"Limitations to standard IPsec","x":3920,"y":2108,"width":587,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"b729645ced9bb3a7","type":"text","text":"1. IPsec does not support broadcast and multicast traffic, only unicast\n\t1. routing protocols such as OSPF can't be used over the tunnels because they rely on multicast traffic. GRE over IPsec solves this\n2. Configuring a full mesh of tunnels between many sites is a labor-intensive task. Takes a lot of time and careful planning.\n\t1. Solved by Cisco's DMVPN","x":3920,"y":2158,"width":587,"height":202},
		{"id":"89967e64a81a7176","type":"text","text":"1. Site-to-site VPNs \n\t1. typically use IPsec\n\t2. VPNs prodive service to many devices within the sites they are connecting\n\t3. VPNs are typically used to permanently connect two sites over the internet\n2. Remote-Access VPNs \n\t1. typically use TLS\n\t2. VPNs provide service to the one ned device the VPN client software is installed on\n\t3. VPNs are typically used to provide on-demand access for end devices that want to securely access company resources while connected to a network which is not secure","x":3393,"y":2085,"width":407,"height":436},
		{"id":"1a0034227c6cf999","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":6322,"y":2707,"width":121,"height":63,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"4b74266c19816b08","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5917,"y":2707,"width":121,"height":63,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"68ea572e148907a1","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5295,"y":2707,"width":121,"height":63},
		{"id":"585a140638ff9c04","type":"text","text":"R (hub)","x":5493,"y":2638,"width":132,"height":63,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"61c76d445115ffa9","type":"text","text":"R (spoke)","x":5714,"y":2707,"width":121,"height":63},
		{"id":"cc6ebe724d4a335e","type":"text","text":"Leased Lines","x":3395,"y":-457,"width":460,"height":50,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"f51ecafa329ba98d","type":"text","text":"- a dedicated physical link, typically connecting two sites.\n- Leased lines use serial connections (PPP/HDLC encapsulation).\n- There are various standards that provide different speeds, and different standards are available in different countries.\n- Due to higher cost, higher installation lead time, and slower speeds of leased lines, Ethernet WAN technologies are becoming more popular","x":3395,"y":-407,"width":460,"height":330},
		{"id":"a06ce846b64e69cf","type":"text","text":"Leased Lines Standards","x":3962,"y":-517,"width":840,"height":50,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"daffd4f600001b69","type":"text","text":"| System                                         | North American                             | Japanese                                                | European                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| Level zero                                     | 64 kbits/s (DS0)                           | 64 kbits/s                                              | 64 kbits/s                              |\n| First Level                                    | 1.544 Mbit/s (DS1) (24 user channels) (T1) | 1.544 Mbit/s (24 user channels)                         | 2.048544 Mbit/s (32 user channels) (E1) |\n| (Intermediate Level, T-carrier hierarchy only) | 3.152 Mbit/s (DS1C) (48 Ch.)               | -                                                       | -                                       |\n| Second Level                                   | 6.312 Mbit/s (DS2) (96 Ch.) (T2)           | 6.312 Mbit/s (DS2) (96 Ch.) or 7.786 Mbit96/s (120 Ch.) | 8.448 Mbit/s (128 Ch.) (E2)             |\n| Third Level                                    | 44.736 Mbit/s (DS3) (672 Ch.) (T3)         | 32.064 Mbit/s (480 Ch.)                                 | 34.368 Mbit/s  (512 Ch.) (E3)           |\n| Fourth Level                                   | 274.176 Mbit/s (DS4) (4032 ch.)            | 97.728 Mbit/s (1440 Ch.)                                | 34.368 Mbit/s  (2048 Ch.) (E3)          |\n| Fifth Level                                    | 400.352 Mbit/s (DS5) (5760 Ch.)            | 565.148 Mbit/s (8192 Ch.)                               | 34.368 Mbit/s  (8192 Ch.) (E3)          |","x":3962,"y":-467,"width":840,"height":427},
		{"id":"d2b814e495ae6c41","type":"text","text":"What are WANs?","x":2580,"y":-538,"width":415,"height":50,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"740c9e8b917120c6","type":"text","text":"<span style=\"font-size: 100px;\">WAN</span>","x":1744,"y":-351,"width":508,"height":220,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"f4977528d4ccf349","type":"text","text":"- Network that extends over a large geographical area\n- Used to connect geographically separate LANs\n- Although the internet itself can be considered a WAN, the term WAN is typically used to refer to an enterprises's private connections that connect their offices, data centers, and other sites together\n- Over public/shared networks like the internet, VPNs (VIrtual Private Networks) can be used to create private WAN connections\n- There have been many different WAN tech over the years. Depending o nthe location, some will be available and some will not be\n- Technologies which are considered 'legacy' in one country might still be used in other countries","x":2590,"y":-487,"width":415,"height":493},
		{"id":"fb077dba377a490d","type":"text","text":"Spine-Leaf Architecture","x":298,"y":2152,"width":284,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6a9a2cc28a4e8935","type":"text","text":"Rules","x":298,"y":2247,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b91bc72b559b6228","type":"text","text":"Access Layer","x":-346,"y":2247,"width":284,"height":60},
		{"id":"d253a158bd402daa","type":"text","text":"Distribution Layer","x":-346,"y":2307,"width":284,"height":60},
		{"id":"4ded0d73aa728ea1","type":"text","text":"Access Layer","x":-5,"y":2247,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"ee277a0d61adc15f","type":"text","text":"Distribution Layer","x":-5,"y":2307,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"709c33fae40194f2","type":"text","text":"Core Layer","x":-5,"y":2367,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5190c0c4a4b6ca12","type":"text","text":"Two-Tier Campus LAN Design","x":-346,"y":2152,"width":284,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"02d5e8796b181a52","type":"text","text":"Three-Tier Campus LAN Design","x":-22,"y":2152,"width":284,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"741bea426247e382","type":"text","text":"SOHO Networks","x":638,"y":2152,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"49d0ad25f823f3c3","type":"text","text":"Common terminologies","x":-960,"y":1962,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"57c0467c676c6378","type":"text","text":"Star","x":-1353,"y":1500,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"60134f54273f7a7c","type":"text","text":"-Several devices all connect to one central device - we can draw them in a start shape","x":-1353,"y":1560,"width":250,"height":160},
		{"id":"2a0df7bf83cb7d91","type":"text","text":"- When each device is connected to each other device","x":-1353,"y":1907,"width":250,"height":160},
		{"id":"5240f4f2cb8bdd14","type":"text","text":"Full Mesh","x":-1353,"y":1847,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"f9c3835bb67faba5","type":"text","text":"Partial Mesh","x":-1360,"y":2162,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"f1bce56a972f2e7b","type":"text","text":"- When some devices are connected to each other, but not all","x":-1360,"y":2222,"width":250,"height":160},
